{"candidates":[{"content":"The isolated artifact DEPENDS ON SRC_002's mathematical formulation of idempotency as f(f(x))=f(x) because the entire distinction between 'idempotent writer' and 'safe writer' for append-only logs is grounded in the formal observation that appending is intrinsically non-idempotent at the byte level. Any alternative definition of idempotency that does not evaluate f(f(x)) against the original x collapses the distinction into a semantic quibble and makes the safe-vs-idempotent taxonomy useless for system design. REFUTED IF the isolated artifact's argument holds without reference to the f(f(x))=f(x) formalism—for instance, if it derives the distinction purely from operational retry semantics or delivery guarantees without invoking the mathematical property at all.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"b5164be7cdd67d07a0068b92fde7104efe5e1ea51d512a94ac8fe192943ef2ed"},{"role":"dependence","target":"15769cf44a857862dbb4eb441ad47f0f8480c52d4858d5bad5f410615b2198f9"},{"role":"dependence","target":"d3d4e3525751db298c73db50cf310be14e4f9a9783fdfe98c15a9b011b67591c"}],"typicality":0.82},{"content":"The isolated artifact SHARES MECHANISM with SRC_001's three-archetype framework, specifically the deduplication-key mechanism that converts a non-idempotent byte-level append into a replay-safe semantic operation. Both artifacts identify the same causal pathway: deterministic identity attached to each event allows duplicate detection, which in turn makes at-least-once delivery survivable. The shared mechanism is the keying step: without it, neither safety nor effective idempotency is achievable for an append-only log. REFUTED IF the isolated artifact achieves replay safety through a fundamentally different mechanism—for example, by making the replay engine stateful (keeping a cursor of already-processed entries across replays) rather than through per-event deterministic keys, which would mean the mechanism is cursor-based deduplication rather than content-based deduplication.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"d3d4e3525751db298c73db50cf310be14e4f9a9783fdfe98c15a9b011b67591c"},{"role":"dependence","target":"a81e30ae284bb4cc0fd947a10392dbbbf32bd8a0dff18a617249a48f8c6a66a2"},{"role":"dependence","target":"e9c1886802e47b71f4e8c19b9907416f0fe5e7c53688249273b1190d1da1e2f4"}],"typicality":0.75},{"content":"The isolated artifact CONTRADICTS SRC_004's claim that append-only logs require 'effectively idempotent' writers. SRC_004 argues that the composite operation 'ensure-this-event-is-in-the-log' is idempotent at the semantic level, collapsing safety and idempotency into a unified property. The isolated artifact insists this collapse is illegitimate: the composite operation is safe but not idempotent, because idempotency is a property of a function's mathematical behavior, not of the resulting state's cardinality. Calling it 'effectively idempotent' obscures the real engineering trade-off between writers that are safe-but-non-idempotent (random UUID) and safe-and-idempotent (deterministic hash). REFUTED IF the isolated artifact cannot produce a concrete case where 'effective idempotency' and 'safety' yield different engineering prescriptions—for if they always prescribe the same writer behavior, the distinction is purely terminological and SRC_004's unification is pragmatically correct even if not formally precise.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"e9c1886802e47b71f4e8c19b9907416f0fe5e7c53688249273b1190d1da1e2f4"},{"role":"dependence","target":"15769cf44a857862dbb4eb441ad47f0f8480c52d4858d5bad5f410615b2198f9"},{"role":"dependence","target":"d3d4e3525751db298c73db50cf310be14e4f9a9783fdfe98c15a9b011b67591c"}],"typicality":0.45},{"content":"The isolated artifact ABSTRACTS from SRC_003's concrete content-addressable and sequence-numbered append mechanisms to a general principle: safety of an append-only log is a property of replay semantics, not of the write primitive. The specific deduplication strategy (content hash, producer+sequence, UUID) is an implementation detail; the abstract principle is that replay must produce the same reasoning state regardless of duplicate physical entries. This abstraction explains why all three of SRC_001's archetypes (random UUID, deterministic hash, global counter) can be evaluated on a single axis: does the replay engine's deduplication logic, combined with the writer's keying strategy, yield a deterministic post-replay state? REFUTED IF the isolated artifact's abstraction turns out to be too coarse—i.e., if different deduplication strategies produce materially different correctness properties under partial failure or concurrent writes, so that 'safety is a replay property' conceals real distinctions between content-hash deduplication and sequence-number deduplication that matter for system correctness.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"a81e30ae284bb4cc0fd947a10392dbbbf32bd8a0dff18a617249a48f8c6a66a2"},{"role":"dependence","target":"b5164be7cdd67d07a0068b92fde7104efe5e1ea51d512a94ac8fe192943ef2ed"},{"role":"dependence","target":"d3d4e3525751db298c73db50cf310be14e4f9a9783fdfe98c15a9b011b67591c"}],"typicality":0.68},{"content":"The isolated artifact REDUCES TO SRC_005's core distinction: idempotency asks whether f(f(x)) = f(x) when the second f receives the original x, while safety asks whether applying f again after it has already mutated state to f(x) causes harm. The isolated artifact's treatment of writer safety for append-only logs reduces to this single distinction because the 'harm' in question is duplicate entries corrupting deterministic replay, and the 'original state' vs 'mutated state' difference is exactly what makes appending non-idempotent but potentially safe. Every engineering prescription in the neighbourhood follows from applying this one conceptual fork to the concrete case of append-only event logs under at-least-once delivery. REFUTED IF the isolated artifact invokes additional conceptual machinery beyond the original-state-vs-mutated-state distinction—for example, if it requires notions of causality, partial ordering, or consensus that are not derivable from SRC_005's formulation alone, showing the reduction is incomplete.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"15769cf44a857862dbb4eb441ad47f0f8480c52d4858d5bad5f410615b2198f9"},{"role":"dependence","target":"b5164be7cdd67d07a0068b92fde7104efe5e1ea51d512a94ac8fe192943ef2ed"},{"role":"dependence","target":"a81e30ae284bb4cc0fd947a10392dbbbf32bd8a0dff18a617249a48f8c6a66a2"}],"typicality":0.72},{"content":"The isolated artifact is COMPATIBLE WITH SRC_001's archetype framework but extends it by identifying a fourth archetype missing from the original taxonomy: the 'idempotency-key writer' that appends a deterministic key separate from the payload (e.g., a request ID). This archetype is safe and effectively idempotent but, unlike the deterministic-hash writer, does not collapse genuinely distinct logical events that happen to share identical content. SRC_001's countercondition already gestures at this case (two distinct payments of the same amount), but does not elevate it to an archetype. The isolated artifact argues this fourth archetype is the practically recommended one, subsuming both the UUID writer (too weak—duplicates are safe but not deduplicated) and the hash writer (too strong—collapses distinct events). REFUTED IF the idempotency-key writer is merely a parameterization of the deterministic-hash archetype (key = hash of a tuple including a nonce) rather than a structurally distinct writer strategy, in which case SRC_001's three-archetype taxonomy is complete and the fourth 'archetype' adds no independent design choice.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"d3d4e3525751db298c73db50cf310be14e4f9a9783fdfe98c15a9b011b67591c"},{"role":"dependence","target":"e9c1886802e47b71f4e8c19b9907416f0fe5e7c53688249273b1190d1